| editorial =
'''The briefcase and the system'''

Nishikawa Daigo carried 14 months of evidence in a briefcase. He waited until the chairman was arrested. He was afraid. That fear is the system — a system where the truth stays locked until someone else takes the fall.

The workers at Akamatsu Group spent three years paying into a pension fund that was being emptied. The system did not see it. The SESC did not audit it. The board did not question it. The whistleblower saw it. Nishikawa documented it. Both waited. Both were afraid. The system that should have protected them produced fear instead.

The IAA Commission is resourced. The audit begins Monday. These are good things. But the pattern is clear: the system does not find the truth. People do. Afraid people. People with briefcases.

